node9 — Node-compatible JavaScript runtime for 9front (QuickJS-ng). Changelog.
(`pac9 changelog node9` shows this; newest first.)

0.2.1 — 2026-07-26
  Interactive use of the real `pi` agent, fixed properly. Every item here was a
  session-ending bug found with pi driving the runtime for real.

    - A tool call no longer kills the keyboard. This was the big one, and it was in
      the cc9 runtime under us: fork() hands the child a copy of the worker-thread
      registry, whose pids belong to the PARENT, and a child that exits without
      exec'ing posts "kill" notes to every one of them. pi starts each tool call with
      spawnSync("which","bash") — 9front has neither — so every tool call killed the
      poll thread feeding stdin. The window kept drawing and the model kept answering
      while typing did nothing.
    - Emoji no longer drift the cursor. The ES2024 /v regex flag did not imply /u, so
      the matcher walked UTF-16 code UNITS and \p{Surrogate} matched half an astral
      character: a TUI measuring text lost the emoji entirely (0 columns instead of 2)
      and each streaming redraw restarted two columns further right.
    - \p{...} parses under /v at all. It only worked under /u, so the property NAME
      became a set of literal characters — a width function then measured every styled
      string short, padded past the terminal width, and its redraws stacked up as rows
      repeating the one above with a few more words on them.
    - A real child_process: pipes, captured output, exit codes, stdin input, cwd/env,
      streamed stdout/stderr, exec/execFile collecting both. "ignore" means /dev/null
      (it used to INHERIT, handing a child the terminal's keyboard). node -v/-e/-p work.
    - stdin survives: an interrupted read (EINTR) is no longer fatal, backpressure no
      longer disarms the reader, and a resize reaches the app — /env is frozen at exec
      on this platform, so the terminal now also publishes the live size to a file
      ($A9_SIZE_FILE) and process.on('SIGWINCH') fires off it.
    - readline.cursorTo/moveCursor/clearLine/clearScreenDown emit real escape
      sequences instead of doing nothing.

0.2.0 — 2026-07-25
  The engine is now built with cc9/clang instead of kencc, TLS certificates are
  verified, and the real `pi` coding agent runs on it — TUI included.

    - New engine build (port/cc9). kencc cannot do computed-goto dispatch, so
      the bytecode loop ran on a `switch`; clang's build is 3.3x faster on the
      interpreter and 2.8x on JS compute in the full runtime. Startup gains
      less (a `pi --version` goes 7.7s -> 6.5s, a full turn 9.0s -> 7.7s):
      module resolution, I/O and parsing dominate there, not interpretation.
    - TLS certificates are VERIFIED. The engine now links OpenSSL and checks the
      chain plus the hostname against /amd64/lib/node9/ca.pem (shipped here).
      The previous build used Plan 9's libsec and verified nothing.
      NODE9_CA_FILE overrides the bundle; NODE9_TLS_INSECURE disables checking.
    - Fixed silent truncation of large reads. cc9's poll layer keyed its read
      ring on a live reader thread, but that thread exits the moment the kernel
      gives it EOF — often with the tail of the transfer still in the ring, which
      then got dropped: a 220 KB HTTP body arrived as 4 KB.
    - Fixed three socket bugs. Ending a request wrote "hangup" to the TCP ctl fd
      as a half-close — Plan 9 has no half-close, so it tore down the connection
      the response was still arriving on. Destroying a TLS socket cleared its fd
      before disarming the read handler, so an aborted fetch left the event loop
      polling a closed fd forever. And a response that ended mid-body (bad chunk
      size, or a close before the last chunk) was reported as a complete body
      instead of an error, which hid all of the above as short reads.
    - Every ready fd is dispatched. quickjs-libc's poll loop overwrote its fd
      count with poll()'s ready count and then scanned only that many leading
      entries, so a ready socket at a later index waited for an unrelated fd to
      go ready too: streamed replies stalled for 80s at a time. Two smaller
      event-loop fixes came with it (an expired timer returned before polling at
      all; an entry module with top-level await advanced one microtask per timer).
    - Real terminal support: a TTY is reported honestly, terminal size comes from
      $LINES/$COLS (what alacritty9 publishes), Return is translated to CR while
      the console is in raw mode, and cooked mode is restored on exit. Enough for
      pi's full-screen TUI under alacritty9 or `drawterm -G`.
    - A real `child_process`: pipes, captured stdout/stderr, exit codes, stdin input,
      cwd/env, streamed output for spawn(). It used to run the command and capture
      nothing, so a tool waiting on a child's output waited forever — that is what
      hung pi's shell tool. Reaping was fixed underneath it too: a child's exit was
      collected by a thread reading /proc/<pid>/wait, which on 9front delivers one
      message to a foreign reader and then stops, so every child after the first was
      never reaped.
    - The shell that gets reported is one that can be driven. 9front's $SHELL is rc,
      which does not speak `sh -c` or `$?`; node9 now points os.userInfo().shell,
      $SHELL and a bare "sh"/"bash" at APE's Bourne shell. pi's shell tool works.
    - `node -v`, `node -e` and `node -p` work. The CLI treated argv[1] as a file
      unconditionally, so every `node -e` (npm lifecycle scripts, configure-style
      checks) failed with "No such file or directory".
    - Web platform coverage for real npm packages: fetch/Headers/Response/
      ReadableStream/FormData/AbortSignal, a builtin `undici` shim, the full
      console family, Intl (Segmenter/Collator/NumberFormat/DateTimeFormat),
      a real process.stdin, fs.watch/watchFile, and Node's ESM/CJS interop rules
      including wildcard "exports" subpaths.

0.1.0 — 2026-07-01
  First release: QuickJS-ng with a Node-compatible standard library, and real
  npm 10.9.8 running on 9front.
